# scoursh scanner configuration - rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.6.1
#
# Exactly one record, whose id is the frozen literal `scanner`.  Every other key
# is optional and takes the stated default, so an absent file is equivalent to
# one containing only `id: scanner`.
#
# Values are DATA and are never expanded or executed: `$HOME` here is five
# literal bytes (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 26).
#
# THE NETWORK KEYS BELOW ARE CLAMPED FOR A RUNNING-ENDPOINT (DAST) SCAN.
# `requests-per-second`, `request-budget`, `circuit-breaker-failures` and
# `circuit-breaker-window` are resolved as usual and then held to a
# conservative module limit inside lib/http.sh, at the same chokepoint the
# scope gate lives at, because the host this tool is pointed at is one it
# cannot vet (docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md, "Safety defaults and authorisation").
# The effective DAST values are 4 requests/second and a per-run budget of
# 5000, so the `request-budget: 20000` below is the schema default for the run
# as a whole and is NOT what a DAST scan will actually spend: a DAST scan is
# clamped to 5000 whatever this line says.  That clamp is SILENT, because the
# value it clamps is the one an operator who never edited this file already
# has; a network run states the numbers it will actually keep on one
# informational line, and warns only about a value someone deliberately raised
# above a limit.  The rate and breaker numbers below already equal their
# limits, so they are never clamped as shipped.
#
# `circuit-breaker-window` is bounded at BOTH ends: shorter than 60 seconds is
# raised to 60 (a shorter window counts fewer failures towards the same
# threshold, which is the weaker breaker), and anything longer than 86400 is
# lowered to a day, which for any real run is the same thing as a window that
# never expires.
#
# The limits are not removable here: there is no config key, environment
# variable, or flag that switches the budget or the breaker off, and no
# schema-valid value of any of these four keys can disable either of them.
#
# RAISING one of them is a different question from removing it, and the answer
# depends on WHERE the raised value came from.  A value set in THIS FILE, or
# left at its default, is clamped down to the conservative limit with one
# warning; a value given explicitly on the command line or in a
# SCOURSH_CONFIG_* environment variable is refused outright (exit 2), because
# scoursh does not run at a number other than the one you asked for.  To
# actually raise one, affirm that you own the target:
#
#   scan.sh dast --target NAME --i-own-target NAME
#
# That affirmation is per run and is never persisted: there is deliberately no
# key here that means "always unrestricted", and there never will be.  It also
# raises nothing by itself - it is a key that makes the higher settings
# available, not a switch that selects one.
#
# `contact` is what a target owner who notices your traffic uses to reach you.
# Every request carries `scoursh/<version> (+<contact>)`; with no contact set,
# the request still identifies the tool but cannot identify you.  One printable
# token, no spaces - an email address or a URL.  `--contact` is the flag form.
#
#   contact: security@example.org

id: scanner
requests-per-second: 4
jobs: 4
http-timeout: 20
max-redirects: 5
request-budget: 20000
circuit-breaker-failures: 10
circuit-breaker-window: 60
fail-on: none
min-confidence: low
redact-secrets: true
formats: json
formats: sarif
formats: html
formats: md
max-matches-per-file: 200
evidence-max-bytes: 512
state-retain-runs: 30
history-window-days: 365
history-max-commits: 5000
lock-stale-seconds: 30
mutex-timeout-seconds: 120
notes: scratch-dir is best pointed at a tmpfs. Overwrite-based erasure is not
  effective on APFS, journalled ext4, or any SSD with wear levelling, so a
  memory-backed scratch directory is the real control for the raw matched lines
  scan_match writes there (docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 9, finding F16).
